WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:44.260
[Music]

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:55.200
[Donald Duck yawning.]

00:00:55.206 --> 00:00:59.239
[The audience laughs.]

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:09.472
[Quacking/talking.]

00:01:09.473 --> 00:01:12.806
[Music]

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:23.839
[Donald continues to talk.]

00:01:23.839 --> 00:01:29.739
[Laughter.]

00:01:29.739 --> 00:01:32.505
[Donald:] Lalalalalahlalalah...

00:01:32.506 --> 00:01:38.972
[Donald bangs his head, audience laughs.]

00:01:38.973 --> 00:01:50.273
[Donald struggles to get comfortable in bed.]

00:01:50.273 --> 00:02:19.573
[Clock ticks.]

00:02:19.573 --> 00:02:33.506
[Growing laughter in the audience. Clock ticks.]

00:02:33.506 --> 00:02:38.872
[He scoots the clock into a drawer to muffle the ticking.]

00:02:38.873 --> 00:02:52.173
[Loud ticking as drawer rattles.]

00:02:52.173 --> 00:02:57.139
[Donald:] I'll show you who's boss.

00:02:57.139 --> 00:02:59.000
[He slams the clock against the wall.]

00:03:04.039 --> 00:03:11.500
[Laughter, ticking, quacking.]

00:03:16.539 --> 00:03:18.405
[Crashing, thumping sound]

00:03:18.406 --> 00:03:20.700
[Donald's bed folds up with him inside.]

00:03:22.773 --> 00:03:27.806
[Donald:] Come on now get going, come on now get tough, get tough, get tough.

00:03:27.806 --> 00:03:31.006
[The crowd erupts with more laughter.]

00:03:31.006 --> 00:03:35.200
[Donald mumbles to himself as the audience laughs.]

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.172
[Donald:] Haaaa, at last.

00:03:44.173 --> 00:03:51.073
[The clock reassembles itself, ticking loudly. The alarm rings.]

00:03:51.073 --> 00:03:58.573
[Springs pop off the bed, making bouncing, springing sounds as the ringing alarm continues.]

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:06.073
[Vaguely middle-eastern music.]

00:04:07.606 --> 00:04:12.600
[The crowd laughs at the movie.]

00:04:18.606 --> 00:04:24.906
[Man 1:] Boy, that part about the clock was really...what's the matter chum, didn't you like it?

00:04:24.906 --> 00:04:28.439
[Buntz:] What's so funny? You're one of those slap-happy guys.

00:04:28.439 --> 00:04:31.239
You sleep like a babe, I suppose.

00:04:31.239 --> 00:04:33.072
[Man 1:] Sure. [Buntz:] Okay, so you're lucky.

00:04:33.073 --> 00:04:35.573
[Man 1:] Okay, so I'm lucky, so what?

00:04:35.573 --> 00:04:36.273
[Buntz:]  So you're lucky, that's all!

00:04:36.273 --> 00:04:42.773
[Sounds of men leaving the room, walking out into the hall.]

00:04:42.773 --> 00:04:44.539
[Man 1:] Well, why can't you sleep?

00:04:44.539 --> 00:04:47.572
[Buntz:] I don't know, I don't know.

00:04:47.573 --> 00:04:49.000
[Footsteps as men continue to exit.]

00:04:54.073 --> 00:04:57.806
[Man 1:] Maybe you should take a warm shower before you hit the sack, Buntz.

00:04:57.806 --> 00:04:59.272
Maybe you don't eat right.

00:04:59.273 --> 00:05:01.706
Could be the detail you run is too tough.

00:05:01.706 --> 00:05:03.839
You know what you need is...

00:05:03.839 --> 00:05:17.105
[Music]

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.300
[Man 1:] Give a hand, huh boy?

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.272
So why don't you try it, huh Buntz?

00:05:27.273 --> 00:05:29.739
[Buntz:] Oh, forget it, will you?

00:05:29.739 --> 00:05:33.639
[Man 1:] Ohhh, I don't know what's come over ya, it's a cinch you don't get any easier to live with.

00:05:33.639 --> 00:05:35.805
[Buntz:] So what.

00:05:35.806 --> 00:05:38.400
[Man 1:] So you don't have to be so touchy.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.300
Come on, Buntz, be yourself.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.205
All right, so she hasn't written for a couple of weeks.

00:05:50.206 --> 00:05:51.172
[Buntz:] Three weeks.

00:05:51.173 --> 00:05:52.773
[Officer:] Does that mean anything?

00:05:52.773 --> 00:05:55.839
Don't worry so much. Don't be a worry-wart.

00:05:55.839 --> 00:05:58.900
Look at me, I never worry.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:02.100
[Sound of water running as shower is turned on.]

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:05.680
What does it get you, worrying all the time?

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:08.100
Do I worry?

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.172
How am I gonna get those leave papers signed by Saturday night?

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:16.673
I could worry about that.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.339
I do, kind of. But not enough to stew all night.

00:06:21.339 --> 00:06:25.505
[Water sounds.]

00:06:25.506 --> 00:06:28.672
Remember that time we were moving in on Saipan?

00:06:28.673 --> 00:06:30.539
Didn't sleep a wink.

00:06:30.539 --> 00:06:32.905
Who did?

00:06:32.906 --> 00:06:35.200
Wasn't anything I could do about it.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.972
Sure, there've been times I couldn't sleep.

00:06:44.973 --> 00:06:49.406
School, the night before exams, first time I ever went to the dentist.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.106
Wider please, this will hurt you a little.

00:06:52.106 --> 00:06:54.339
Nuts!

00:06:54.340 --> 00:07:00.620
Things like that''ll keep you awake maybe. But you, you stay awake night after night.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.140
Don't seem to have anything on your mind.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.500
If you do, you don't tell a guy about it.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:18.500
[Sounds of water running.]

00:07:18.506 --> 00:07:23.772
You know, I, I don't get it.

00:07:23.773 --> 00:07:28.300
Well, maybe you'll really get some shuteye tonight, huh? What do you say?

00:07:29.300 --> 00:08:00.072
[Wristwatch ticking, low music in background.]

00:08:00.073 --> 00:08:01.000
[Buntz:] Go to sleep.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.000
Go to sleep.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:10.000
Why can't I get some sleep?

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:13.372
Night after night.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:15.739
I can't take it.

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:18.905
I'd rather be dead.

00:08:21.872 --> 00:08:22.372
Probably will die.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:30.100
Nobody, nobody can last without sleep.

00:08:31.173 --> 00:08:33.700
Night after night.

00:08:34.739 --> 00:08:38.572
Night after night after night.

00:08:38.573 --> 00:08:41.273
[Narrator:] Oh no, you're not gonna die.

00:08:41.273 --> 00:08:48.573
We know how you feel, but in all medical history, nobody's ever died from lack of sleep.

00:08:48.573 --> 00:08:54.206
Before that can happen, nature will take over and give you the rest and sleep that you need,

00:08:54.206 --> 00:08:56.600
whether you realize it or not.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:59.720
But you're worried.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:06.539
You've got something on your mind, and that's what keeps you lying there, staring at the ceiling.

00:09:06.539 --> 00:09:10.072
Everybody does that once in a while.

00:09:10.073 --> 00:09:12.573
Look at Lucky right now.

00:09:12.573 --> 00:09:14.806
He's still awake.

00:09:14.806 --> 00:09:16.772
Insomnia?

00:09:16.773 --> 00:09:20.973
Well, that's only a vague medical term.

00:09:20.973 --> 00:09:23.806
Let's see what's going on in his mind.

00:09:23.806 --> 00:09:27.372
Each man's problem is more or less different.

00:09:27.373 --> 00:09:31.206
But here is one kind. Listen.

00:09:31.206 --> 00:09:33.772
[Lucky:] Dammit to hell. What's the matter with me?

00:09:33.773 --> 00:09:37.173
I got to get some sleep.

00:09:37.173 --> 00:09:42.273
What goes on, I'm all wound up like a top talking to that guy.

00:09:42.273 --> 00:09:46.206
Oh, fine. Now I can't sleep.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:48.606
[Narrator:] Wound up like a top.

00:09:48.606 --> 00:09:51.706
Yes, that's a good way to put it.

00:09:51.706 --> 00:10:03.472
And getting wound up, excitement, anticipation, minor indecisions, confusion, anything like that is a sure way to postpone sleep.

00:10:03.473 --> 00:10:08.739
You have to unwind before you can rest. Listen to him.

00:10:08.739 --> 00:10:12.939
[Lucky:] How am I going to get those papers put through?

00:10:12.939 --> 00:10:16.272
I don't know why I had to get myself in the doghouse with the chief.

00:10:18.473 --> 00:10:18.973
I wonder what's eating Buntz.

00:10:18.973 --> 00:10:21.639
Hell, I can't help him.

00:10:21.639 --> 00:10:25.700
It's the doc's job. Sure, see the doc.

00:10:27.706 --> 00:10:33.506
[Narrator:] It goes on and on, and the more wound up he gets, the worse it is.

00:10:33.506 --> 00:10:35.406
That's just tension.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:41.839
He doesn't have to do this. Tension, that's all.

00:10:41.839 --> 00:10:46.239
But he won't get any real rest when he's all wound up like that.

00:10:46.239 --> 00:10:49.705
Let's see what he does about it.

00:10:49.706 --> 00:10:54.139
[Lucky:] All right, I can't sleep.

00:10:54.139 --> 00:10:56.105
So why can't you sleep?

00:10:56.106 --> 00:10:57.872
Eat too much?

00:10:57.873 --> 00:10:59.439
Too much coffee?

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:01.105
Uh-uh.

00:11:01.106 --> 00:11:03.606
Work too hard?

00:11:03.606 --> 00:11:05.206
Who me?

00:11:05.206 --> 00:11:07.906
Naw, not there. Yeah.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:10.300
That's okay.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.200
Aw, what the hell?

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:19.200
I wonder if I'll have a clean shirt?

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:22.080
Borrow one.

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:26.039
Buntz ought to see the doc.

00:11:26.039 --> 00:11:31.672
Maybe I can...oh, go to sleep.

00:11:31.673 --> 00:11:33.373
Forget it.

00:11:33.373 --> 00:11:35.573
Forget the papers.

00:11:35.573 --> 00:11:37.239
Forget Buntz.

00:11:37.239 --> 00:11:38.672
It's night.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:42.400
It's a problem for the morning.

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:47.800
It's night.

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.200
[Narrator:] Get the idea?

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:56.440
Here's the whole business in a nutshell.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:00.039
At first, his thoughts had him on a merry-go-round.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:05.000
Then he took control, pinned down the cause.

00:12:05.006 --> 00:12:09.639
He was stewing around about you, then about his leave.

00:12:09.639 --> 00:12:16.872
But once he decided what his troubles were, then decided he couldn't do anything about them tonight, that was that.

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:20.800
He began to relax, and look at him now.

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:25.100
Lucky's method won't work for you, will it?

00:12:25.973 --> 00:12:28.839
Your problem isn't quite that easy.

00:12:28.839 --> 00:12:34.672
If it were as simple as one, two, three, you wouldn't be lying awake night after night.

00:12:34.673 --> 00:12:37.306
Maybe you know what's bothering you.

00:12:37.306 --> 00:12:39.972
And maybe you don't exactly.

00:12:39.973 --> 00:12:42.406
That happens to lots of people.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:50.906
Sometimes they can't put their finger right on the cause, and sometimes they can but refuse to admit it,

00:12:50.906 --> 00:12:53.672
even to themselves.

00:12:53.673 --> 00:12:58.439
You know, we're all a little different.

00:12:58.439 --> 00:13:04.100
It's interesting to look around just one ward and see just how different we are.

00:13:05.139 --> 00:13:14.705
The kind of person we were to start with, and how we've been brought up may make a difference in the way we react to our problems.

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:21.680
Maybe it's a question of marriage, money, or a job.

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:28.360
Maybe it's a question of getting along with others, and again,

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:35.240
sometimes it's a question of getting along with ourselves, or just plain lack of purpose.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:37.600
Maybe it's a combination.

00:13:38.673 --> 00:13:46.239
It may be purely physical, like a toothache or a recent operation, but whatever it is, there's no cure-all.

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:51.539
Every guy's worry is a personal problem.

00:13:51.539 --> 00:13:57.805
If each one of these men had a stomachache, would you give him the same dose of medicine?

00:13:57.806 --> 00:14:00.039
No, of course not.

00:14:00.039 --> 00:14:02.172
They're all different.

00:14:02.173 --> 00:14:04.939
You'd tell him to see the doctor and get individual attention.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:07.939
That's what you ought to do tomorrow.

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:11.872
Tell the doc what's on your mind.

00:14:11.873 --> 00:14:15.706
But we can give you some help right now.

00:14:15.706 --> 00:14:18.606
What detail you on, Buntz?

00:14:18.606 --> 00:14:20.739
[Buntz:] Maintenance squad.

00:14:20.739 --> 00:14:22.772
[Narrator:] Grounds?

00:14:22.773 --> 00:14:25.006
[Buntz:] Cars and machines.

00:14:25.006 --> 00:14:28.100
[Narrator:] Oh, um-hum. Responsible work.

00:14:29.139 --> 00:14:33.000
Now, think back over the jobs you did today.

00:14:34.073 --> 00:14:37.506
[Buntz:] I spent most of the day on my back under a truck.

00:14:37.506 --> 00:14:42.506
[Narrator:] Feeling something like a spring that's wound up too tight?

00:14:42.506 --> 00:14:46.006
[Buntz:] Well, yeah.

00:14:46.006 --> 00:14:49.706
Kind of tied up in knots at the back of the neck.

00:14:49.706 --> 00:14:53.172
[Narrator:] The back of the neck, elbows, and chest?

00:14:53.173 --> 00:14:56.100
[Buntz:] Yeah. It's funny.

00:14:57.106 --> 00:15:03.672
I lie there fiddling with that screwdriver, and the first thing I know, I'm trying to figure things out.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.939
I get to talking away.

00:15:07.939 --> 00:15:13.172
Not out loud, you understand, but my jaws are working.

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:20.020
[Narrator:] And here in the ward you go on doing the same thing, trying to work things out in your head

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:24.373
about the way Lucky was doing a few minutes ago.

00:15:24.373 --> 00:15:26.873
[Buntz:] Yeah.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.673
[Narrator:] Did you ever wind a watch too tight?

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:35.600
You have to wind it, but if you wind it too tight, the spring freezes tight, and the watch stops.

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:42.600
The right amount of tension keeps the watch, or you, ticking along in good shape.

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:46.839
You're too tense all day long.

00:15:46.839 --> 00:15:51.005
At chow, or just walking from one building to another.

00:15:51.006 --> 00:15:55.972
You're wound up tight all the time, day and night.

00:15:55.973 --> 00:16:00.873
You don't think about it during the day because something's always going on.

00:16:00.873 --> 00:16:07.406
But at night, you keep right on generating energy with no good way to get rid of it.

00:16:07.406 --> 00:16:08.806
Result?

00:16:08.806 --> 00:16:11.772
You not only lie awake, but you worry about being awake.

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:20.039
Now, see if we can't channel that energy, so even if you are awake, you get some rest.

00:16:20.039 --> 00:16:27.272
Let's start with your head, and relax gradually, all the way down.

00:16:27.273 --> 00:16:29.139
[Buntz:] Well that's easy to say, but--

00:16:29.139 --> 00:16:31.439
[Narrator:] How do you do it?

00:16:31.439 --> 00:16:33.105
Well, get this first.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:37.506
Relaxing's a skill, like hitting a target.

00:16:37.506 --> 00:16:42.039
It takes practice, concentration, and more practice.

00:16:42.039 --> 00:16:45.972
Go ahead, start getting comfortable right now.

00:16:45.973 --> 00:16:51.606
Eventually, relaxing becomes automatic, and you can do it without thinking.

00:16:51.606 --> 00:16:53.706
[Buntz:] I hope you're right.

00:16:53.706 --> 00:16:55.206
[Narrator:] Um-hum.

00:16:55.206 --> 00:16:57.339
To begin with, stretch out flat on the bed.

00:16:57.339 --> 00:17:01.472
Get into a position that suits you best.

00:17:01.473 --> 00:17:04.539
That's better. Straighten out.

00:17:04.539 --> 00:17:06.239
Okay.

00:17:06.239 --> 00:17:09.805
Lie quietly but don't force yourself to hold still.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:18.139
If you are at the control panel, imagine you're going to switch off the current to the tight places, one knob at a time.

00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:21.272
Slow and easy.

00:17:21.273 --> 00:17:24.973
Start by turning off that worried look.

00:17:24.973 --> 00:17:28.539
Ease into a smile, not a smirk.

00:17:28.539 --> 00:17:31.072
Loosen the furrows on your forehead.

00:17:31.073 --> 00:17:35.139
Now around your nose and between your eyes.

00:17:35.139 --> 00:17:46.472
Your mouth is relaxed, and already there's less tension in your shoulders, less tension in your arms, your fists, loosen them up, one at a time.

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:49.500
Take a good breath.

00:17:50.573 --> 00:17:55.073
Now yawn. If you don't feel yawning, fake one.

00:17:55.073 --> 00:17:58.506
Again.

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:01.739
Next, relieve that pressure from your neck.

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:07.873
Think of it as pulling out a row of stitches, unraveling them, one at a time.

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:13.273
Then your back muscles, relax them too.

00:18:13.273 --> 00:18:18.200
Let the pressure off the thigh muscles.

00:18:19.206 --> 00:18:22.206
Take the knots out of the calf muscles.

00:18:22.206 --> 00:18:29.039
Relax the right leg, left leg, then the ankles, one at a time.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:31.800
Your feet.

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:33.060
Your toes.

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:37.340
Remember that sock in the Donald Duck picture?

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:41.040
Imagine it dropped, just plop.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:44.539
That's how you should lie there.

00:18:44.539 --> 00:18:47.305
Plopped out, limp.

00:18:47.306 --> 00:18:50.806
And now, will you sleep?

00:18:50.806 --> 00:18:55.100
Well, if you can't, the doctor will help you.

00:18:55.739 --> 00:19:00.800
But at least you'll be in condition to pass the night restfully and comfortably.

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:16.840
[Music]

